title: Urban Tree Canopy and Summer Surface Temperature
authors: M. Okonkwo · R. Almeida · K. Sato
lang: en
---

:::abstract
We surveyed tree-canopy cover and midday surface temperature across forty neighbourhood blocks in a
mid-sized city over one summer. Blocks above 30% canopy ran 4–7 °C cooler at the pavement than blocks
below 10%, and the relationship held after controlling for building density and surface material.
:::

# Introduction {#sec:intro}

Cities accumulate heat through dark, impervious surfaces that absorb solar radiation by day and release
it slowly at night. The result, an urban heat island, raises energy demand, worsens air quality, and
falls hardest on residents without air conditioning. Tree canopy is among the few interventions that
addresses the cause rather than the symptom: shade intercepts radiation before it reaches the ground,
and transpiration moves heat into latent rather than sensible form.

Prior work has mapped canopy and temperature at the scale of whole cities, but block-level variation is
where planting decisions are actually made. A single street's choices — setback width, species, spacing
— determine whether a block is liveable in August. We set out to measure that variation directly.

# Methods {#sec:methods}

We selected forty blocks stratified by building density and existing canopy, then logged surface
temperature with a vehicle-mounted infrared array on three cloudless afternoons. Canopy cover was
derived from leaf-on aerial imagery and verified on foot. Each block contributed a median temperature
and a canopy fraction; we fit a mixed-effects model with density and dominant surface as covariates.

Measurements were taken between 13:00 and 15:00 local time, when surface temperature peaks. We
discarded readings within five metres of a moving vehicle's exhaust plume and averaged the remainder
over a twenty-metre window.

# Results {#sec:results}

Canopy cover ranged from 4% to 52% across the sample. Midday surface temperature fell almost linearly
with canopy up to roughly 35% cover, after which the marginal cooling flattened. Blocks above 30%
canopy averaged 4–7 °C cooler than blocks below 10%, a gap large enough to move a hot afternoon out of
the range associated with heat-related emergency visits.

Building density mattered, but less than canopy: dense blocks with good canopy stayed cool, while open
blocks with little canopy were the hottest in the sample. Surface material — asphalt versus lighter
concrete — shifted the baseline but did not change the slope.

# Discussion {#sec:discussion}

The flattening above 35% suggests diminishing returns: the first trees on a bare block buy far more
cooling than the last. For a planting budget, that argues for spreading effort across many under-treed
blocks rather than concentrating it where canopy is already good.

The effect size is practical, not just statistical. A 5 °C reduction at the pavement is the difference
a pedestrian feels, and it compounds over a season into measurable cuts in cooling energy.

# Conclusion

Block-level canopy is a strong, actionable lever on summer surface temperature. The cooling is largest
where canopy is currently lowest, which is also, in most cities, where vulnerable residents are
concentrated. Targeted planting is therefore both the most effective and the most equitable option.

